// ── FAQ (15, ~150 words each, covers long-tail) ──
const faqItems = [
  { q: "What is the best nature destination in China for first-time visitors?", a: "Guilin and Yangshuo. They combine the two things first-timers want most — iconic scenery and easy logistics. The karst peaks are the landscape you already know from Chinese paintings, the infrastructure is mature (airport, high-speed rail, English-friendly guesthouses), and you can experience it by bamboo raft, bike or on foot with almost no fitness required. Spend 3–4 days, and pair it with Zhangjiajie or a city like Beijing or Shanghai. Jiuzhaigou is more beautiful but harder to reach and altitude is a factor. Zhangjiajie is more dramatic but involves more climbing and queues." },
  { q: "Guilin, Zhangjiajie or Jiuzhaigou — which one should I choose?", a: "Pick by what you value most. Guilin/Yangshuo is the easiest and most relaxing — river cruises, cycling, rice terraces. Zhangjiajie is for drama and adventure — the floating Avatar peaks, glass bridges and cliff walks. Jiuzhaigou is for pure photographic wow — unreal turquoise lakes and waterfalls, but it is remote, seasonal and altitude-affected. If you can only do one: Guilin for ease, Zhangjiajie for spectacle, Jiuzhaigou for color. If you have 10+ days, you can do two of them back-to-back by high-speed rail or a short flight." },
  { q: "How many days do I need for a nature trip in China?", a: "Most single destinations need 2–3 days on the ground. Guilin and Yangshuo: 3–4 days. Zhangjiajie: 3 days. Jiuzhaigou: 2–3 days (plus travel). Huangshan: 2 days. For a multi-stop nature route, budget 5–10 days. China is large, so build in half a day of travel between stops. A realistic 'best of China nature' first trip is 7–10 days covering two regions — for example Guilin + Zhangjiajie, or Chengdu (pandas) + Jiuzhaigou." },
  { q: "What is the best time of year to visit China for nature?", a: "Spring (March–May) and autumn (September–November) are the sweet spots across most of China — mild temperatures, clear skies and the best colors. Autumn (late September to October) is the single best window for lakes and forests, but avoid the first week of October (Golden Week), when everything is packed and prices triple. Summer suits the grasslands and high plateau. Winter is quiet, cheap and misty — great for snow scenery and hot springs but not for lush green landscapes." },
  { q: "Do I need a visa to visit China?", a: "Not necessarily. China currently grants 30-day visa-free entry to citizens of around 40 countries — the UK and Canada were added in 2026 on a trial basis — and roughly 29 more are covered by mutual visa-exemption agreements. A separate 240-hour (10-day) visa-free transit policy applies to travelers from 57 countries (including the US) entering at 65 ports across 24 provinces — Guilin and Nanning are on the list. US citizens flying on to a third country can use the transit option; otherwise they need a tourist (L) visa, $140 for a 10-year multiple-entry. The lists change frequently, so always verify the latest rules for your passport on the official Chinese embassy site before booking, and apply 4–6 weeks ahead if a visa is required." },
  { q: "What is the most underrated natural wonder in China?", a: "Hailuogou Glacier in Sichuan. Everyone chases Zhangjiajie and Jiuzhaigou, but at Hailuogou you stand at the foot of a 1,000m ice fall without ever going above 3,000m — then soak in natural hot springs while looking up at Mount Gongga (7,556m), the highest peak of Sichuan. A close second is the Yunnan Stone Forest: a UNESCO site that feels like another planet, yet most visitors to Kunming never make the easy day trip. Zhangye Danxia at sunset also deserves more credit than its photos suggest." },
  { q: "Can I see giant pandas in the wild?", a: "Seeing a panda in the true wild is extremely rare — even researchers rarely spot them. What travelers actually do is visit panda bases and reserves, where pandas live in large, semi-natural forest enclosures. The best are Chengdu's Panda Base (easy, day trip) and the Dujiangyan Panda Base (volunteer programs, less crowded). Wolong Nature Reserve in Sichuan offers the closest thing to 'wild' viewing with forested mountain enclosures. Go in the early morning (8–10am) when pandas are most active and feeding." },
  { q: "Is it safe to hike in China's national parks?", a: "Yes, on maintained trails. China's major parks — Huangshan, Zhangjiajie, Jiuzhaigou — have boardwalks, stone steps, railings and clear signage. The risks are the same as hiking anywhere: slippery steps after rain, altitude on high routes, and underestimating distances. Stay on marked paths, wear proper shoes, carry water, and avoid off-trail scrambling on remote peaks without a guide. For remote treks (Tiger Leaping Gorge, Hailuogou, Meili Snow Mountain), go with a licensed guide or join a group." },
  { q: "How do I deal with altitude sickness on the Tibetan plateau?", a: "Ascend gradually and give your body time. Fly to a mid-altitude city (Chengdu or Kunming) and acclimatize before going higher. Once above ~3,000m: take it easy for the first 24–48 hours, avoid alcohol and heavy meals, drink plenty of water, and sleep at the lowest altitude you can manage. Over-the-counter acetazolamide (Diamox) helps many travelers when started a day before ascent. If symptoms worsen (severe headache, vomiting, breathlessness), descend — that is the only real cure. Consult a doctor before any high-altitude trip." },
  { q: "Do I need to book national park tickets in advance?", a: "Yes, for the big ones. Jiuzhaigou strictly requires online reservation with a daily visitor cap — buy tickets on the official app or via a hotel days ahead, especially in autumn. Zhangjiajie and Huangshan tickets can often be bought at the gate but sell out in peak season. Golden Week (Oct 1–7) and summer weekends require booking everything in advance. Note that Chinese parks often require passport registration at purchase, and most tickets are now digital QR codes linked to your passport or a Chinese phone app." },
  { q: "How do I get between nature destinations in China?", a: "China's high-speed rail network is the fastest, cheapest and most reliable way to connect destinations. Guilin, Zhangjiajie, Huangshan and Chengdu all have high-speed stations with frequent trains to major cities. Remote areas (Jiuzhaigou, Shangri-La, Kanas) are reached by domestic flights from Chengdu, Kunming or Xi'an. Trains bookable 15 days ahead on Trip.com or the official 12306 app; flights are often cheaper than you expect. Allow half a day for any transfer, and note that many stations are outside the city center." },
  { q: "What should I pack for a China nature trip?", a: "Broken-in walking shoes are non-negotiable — you will climb thousands of steps. Add: layers (mountain mornings are cold even in summer), a light rain jacket, sunscreen and a hat, a power bank (you will navigate and pay by phone), passport and printed visa/hotel copies, any regular medication, and cash as backup (small notes for rural areas). If going above 3,000m, pack a warm layer and altitude medication. Leave room in your bag — you will buy tea, snacks and souvenirs." },
  { q: "Is China safe for solo nature travelers?", a: "China is one of the safest countries for solo travelers — violent crime against tourists is extremely rare, and solo women generally report feeling safe even at night. The real challenges are practical, not safety-related: the language barrier, paying without a Chinese bank card, and navigating apps that require a local number. In remote areas, tell your accommodation your route and expected return time before setting out. Common sense — the same you would use anywhere — is all you need." },
  { q: "Can I use Google Maps and WhatsApp in China?", a: "Google Maps and WhatsApp do not work on Chinese networks without a VPN, and many VPNs are unreliable inside China. The practical fix most travelers use: a travel eSIM from Hong Kong or a roaming data plan, which usually routes around the block and lets Google/WhatsApp/Instagram work normally. For maps, download Amap or Baidu Maps (English support is limited) or use Apple Maps, which works reasonably in China. Set up Alipay/WeChat Pay and download any VPN before you fly." },
  { q: "How much does a nature trip in China cost?", a: "China offers excellent value. A comfortable mid-range nature trip runs about $60–120 per person per day, including a private room, meals, park tickets and transport. Budget travelers can do $30–50/day in hostels. National park entrance tickets are the hidden cost — typically $15–40 per park, plus shuttle buses and cable cars that can double the ticket. Flights and high-speed rail between destinations are the other main expense. A realistic 7-day, two-destination trip is roughly $800–1,500 per person excluding international flights." },
  { q: "Are China's national parks crowded?", a: "The famous ones can be, especially in summer and during Golden Week (Oct 1–7), when domestic tourism surges. Zhangjiajie and Huangshan see long queues for cable cars and viewpoints on peak days. The fix is timing: visit on weekdays, arrive at opening time (most parks open 6–7am), and avoid national holidays. Jiuzhaigou caps daily visitors, so it never feels as chaotic. Dozens of lesser-known parks — Fanjingshan, Kanas, Hailuogou, Xishuangbanna — offer equally stunning scenery with a fraction of the crowds." },
  { q: "What food should I try at China's nature destinations?", a: "Each landscape region has its own kitchen. In Guilin, eat Guilin rice noodles for breakfast and beer fish by the Li River. In Zhangjiajie, try sanxiaguo — a sizzling hotpot of cured meats. Sichuan is hotpot and mapo tofu; Jiuzhaigou adds Tibetan yak dishes. On the Silk Road in Dunhuang, order donkey-meat yellow noodles and lamb soup. In Yunnan, try crossing-the-bridge noodles and Dai-style grilled fish; in Tibet, yak momos and sweet tea. Tipping is not expected anywhere, and meals at the park gate are the worst value — walk into town and eat where locals do." },
  { q: "Where should I stay when visiting parks like Zhangjiajie or Jiuzhaigou?", a: "Stay at the park gate, not in the city. In Zhangjiajie, book in Wulingyuan town by the east gate — staying in Zhangjiajie city adds a 40-minute shuttle each way. In Jiuzhaigou there is no lodging inside the park, so book in Zhangzha village at the entrance. In Huangshan, spend one night on the summit (Beihai or Paiyunlou hotel) to catch sunrise — book weeks ahead. Book 1–2 months early for October Golden Week, Chinese New Year and July–August. Every hotel registers your passport at check-in, which is normal and required." },
  { q: "Is Chinese food spicy? Can I eat vegetarian or halal?", a: "Spice is regional, not universal. Sichuan and Hunan food is famously hot — say 'bù là' (not spicy) to tone it down. Cantonese and Yunnan food are much milder. Vegetarian options exist in cities and at Buddhist temples ('wǒ chī sù' means 'I eat vegetarian'), but rural meat-heavy regions are harder — print an allergy card in Chinese. Halal food is widely available in the northwest (Gansu, Ningxia, Xinjiang), where lamb and noodles dominate. Street food is generally safe if you stick to busy stalls." },
  { q: "What should I wear for China's different landscapes?", a: "Match clothing to climate zone, not just the season. High plateau (Tibet, Shangri-La, Jiuzhaigou): thermals, fleece or down, windproof shell, hat and SPF 50 — cold mornings and fierce sun even in summer. Desert (Dunhuang): loose long sleeves, a scarf for sand, closed shoes — hot days, freezing nights. Subtropical south (Guilin, Zhangjiajie): quick-dry layers and a rain jacket — hot, humid, sudden downpours. Northern grassland (Hulunbuir): layers plus a warm fleece for cold evenings even in July. Broken-in walking shoes, sunscreen and a power bank cover almost everything else." },
  { q: "Do I need to book trains, flights and hotels in advance?", a: "Yes, for anything in peak season. Train tickets open 15 days ahead at 8am Beijing time and popular routes sell out — book via Trip.com or the 12306 app. Domestic flights to remote parks (Jiuzhaigou, Shangri-La, Kanas) are often cheap but should be booked early for holidays. Hotels next to park gates sell out first; book 1–2 months ahead for Golden Week (Oct 1–7), Chinese New Year and July–August. National-park tickets (Jiuzhaigou especially) and Huangshan summit hotels are the scarcest — lock those before anything else." },
];

